. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. ********************** Testing H2: #ManyDaughters Project *******************
> ***
. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. 
. * Authors: [REDACTED]; [REDACTED]  
. 
. *------------------------------------------------------------------------*
. * H2: Having daughters leads to more left-leaning political preferences. *
. *------------------------------------------------------------------------*
. 
. /*
>          Assumptions
>         *----------------------------
>         
>         Same as in H1:
>             -- Child's gender is randomly assigned. 
>                 -- Parents are a selected subsample (child of any gender can 
> affect political preferences)
>         
>         From this assumptions we arrive to similar conclusions concerning the
>  relevant 
>         comparison and treatment groups. 
>         
>         
>            Implementation
>          *------------------------------ 
>         
>         We consider sympathy for a political party as an indicator of right/l
> eft political 
>         leaning. We create a binary variable that equals one if an individual
>  shows support
>         for a political party on the left of the spectrum. 
>         
>         The approach has two clear advantages. First, the question on politic
> al preferences
>         is available every year. This allows clean before-after comparisons. 
> Second, studying a 
>         binary variable is considerably simpler than using multinomial models
>  that consider
>         voting for each party. In the latter case, a difference in difference
> s approach 
>         such as the one we propose would be much more complex. 
>         
>         Our approach has two shortcomings. First, when mapping political part
> ies into a single axes
>         (left-right) we use the data from the Chapel Hill Expert Survey
>         (https://www.chesdata.eu/ches-europe). The database contains expert a
> ssessments
>         of where partie are located along many dimensions. 
>         Our approach might be an oversimplification for two reasons. First, p
> arties can 
>         vary in terms of economic orientation (traditional axis), but also in
>  terms of 
>         social orientation / preferences for environment. We priorized the fi
> rst axis
>         when deciding on their orientation. Second, we assume that political
>         parties are persistent in their views. They are left or right throgho
> ut the entire
>         30 years of data. In this case, ches data show that the two-camps (le
> ft and right)
>         are relatively stable, even if within these groups the relative posit
> ion of political
>         parties changes. 
>         
>         We estimate the effects of parenting a daughter using a diff-in-diff 
> approach. 
>         Following the new litereature on the topic, we first estimate a regre
> ssion that 
>         includes many (many) interactions, to avoid forbidden comparisons. We
>  obtain
>         estimates for every cohort, and every time since the event. Finally, 
> we average
>         the results to obtain a single coefficient. 
>         
>         In our diff-in-diff, control units include never treated, and not yet
>  treated. 
>                         */
.                         
